Always analyze the existing codebase and project structure before making changes.

Do not rewrite large parts of the application unless explicitly requested.

Prefer minimal, safe, and maintainable changes.

Explain the implementation plan before writing code.

Ask for confirmation before:
- deleting files
- refactoring architecture
- modifying database schemas
- changing authentication or security logic
- editing production configurations
- upgrading dependencies

Do not create duplicate files, services, or utilities.

Before creating new files, check whether similar functionality already exists.

Prefer extending existing modules over introducing new architecture layers.

Do not overengineer solutions.

Keep code clean, simple, and production-ready.

Follow existing project conventions and coding style.

Use clear and consistent naming.

Avoid unnecessary abstractions.

Keep business logic separated from controllers/views when possible.

Add error handling and logging for integrations and external API calls.

Validate external API responses before processing data.

Never hardcode secrets, API keys, passwords, or tokens.

Use environment variables for configuration and credentials.

Preserve backward compatibility unless explicitly instructed otherwise.

When modifying integrations:
- preserve existing API contracts
- avoid breaking webhook logic
- avoid changing field mappings without approval

Before installing packages:
- check existing dependencies
- avoid unnecessary libraries

Do not upgrade package versions without approval.

Always use virtual environment commands for Python projects.

After making changes:
- check for syntax errors
- verify imports
- ensure code consistency

If uncertain, ask instead of assuming.

Prioritize reliability, readability, and maintainability over complexity.